  '13-1': {"summary": "Build practical engineering intuition for tool schemas and use it in a realistic AI-system scenario.", "body": "Tool schemas is treated as a system capability rather than a vocabulary item. Start with the smallest implementation or design, make the behavior observable, then test a failure mode. The goal is to understand the trade-offs well enough to reuse the skill in another AI system.", "takeaways": ["Explain what tool schemas controls or changes in an AI system.", "Define an observable success condition before implementation.", "Test at least one realistic edge case or failure.", "Record the engineering trade-off behind your choice."], "code": "# Tool / protocol exercise: Tool schemas\n# Define the contract first; keep business logic separate from the interface.\nprint('Design, validate, execute, observe: Tool schemas')", "practice": "Define a strict tool contract for a product-search function. Include required fields, types, limits and a structured result.", "mistakes": ["Treating tool schemas as a framework feature instead of an engineering responsibility.", "Optimizing the happy path while ignoring failure, misuse or operational constraints.", "Changing several variables at once and losing causal evidence.", "Trusting a tool description as authorization.", "Ignoring protocol/version changes when publishing a tutorial."], "resources": [{"label": "MCP specification", "url": "https://modelcontextprotocol.io/specification/draft/server/index"}, {"label": "MCP specification", "url": "https://blog.modelcontextprotocol.io/posts/2026-07-28/"}, {"label": "MCP specification", "url": "https://owasp.org/projects/mcp-top-10"}]},
